        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 5,
        "titulo": "Review: essere, avere and agreement",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: reflexive verbs, movement to a place, and changes of state take «essere». The participle agrees with the subject: «-o, -a, -i, -e». STEP 2: most other verbs and any non-reflexive verb with a direct object take «avere», and the participle does not change with the subject. STEP 3: «già», «mai» and «appena» go between the auxiliary and the participle. STEP 4: «non… ancora» wraps the auxiliary. STEP 5: «stato» is the participle of «essere» and also of «stare».",
        "comparacao": "The English helper «have» is easier but gives you no clue. In Italian, choosing the helper is part of the meaning, and agreement is part of the spelling. If you doubt a participle, say the sentence aloud with the right ending: the sound usually tells you.",
